#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151889 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/id #uuid "3a4c4912-e478-5332-b088-0df7ac4e8c42" :question/math-academy-id "e-6578" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{3x}$ are solutions to the equation $y'' - 2y - 3y = 0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$\n\n- $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$\n\n- $y(x) =- e^{x} + e^{3x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c_1$ and $c_2$ are constants, then $y(x) = c_1 y_1(x) + c_2 y_2(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that $y_1(x) = e^{-x}$ and $y_2(x) =e^{3x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t) = c_1 e^{-x} + c_2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, $c_1 = 7$ and $c_2 = -6.$\n\n- The function $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation. The superposition principle only applies to linear combinations of known solutions. It does not extend to the divisions of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) = - e^{x} + e^{3x}$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation because $e^x$ is not a solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151923 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "7e533704-ef66-5bab-b151-83345bce320b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6865" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^x$ are solutions to the equation $y''+y'-2y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{-2x}}{e^{x}}$\n\n- $y(x) = e^{-2x}\\cdot e^{x}$\n\n- $y(x) = e^{-2x} +e^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous differential equation, then $y(x) = y_1(x) + y_2(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{-2x}}{e^{x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) = e^{-2x}\\cdot e^{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) =  e^{-2x} +e^{x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151924 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "c4c3ed32-67c8-56a3-9855-4bfa2696bfc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6866" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 4y = 0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = 2xe^{2x}$\n\n- $y(x) = 3e^{2x}$\n\n- $y(x) = -e^{2x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function $y_1(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x) = c y_1(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = 2xe^{2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x) = 3e^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=3.$\n\n- The function $y(x) = -e^{2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "662d1d06-dd17-5de3-83ec-2cedc1760998" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343806} {:answer/id #uuid "d949191f-9f46-55e9-9520-7e4e12abf55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343807} {:answer/id #uuid "8663551c-ab1d-55f1-97d0-860ea6a112c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343808} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf3db7b-9abf-5af5-bcdf-e17ab7417764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343809} {:answer/id #uuid "23cca72b-25f8-5148-9741-215c615b35b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8663551c-ab1d-55f1-97d0-860ea6a112c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343808} :answer-field/id #uuid "24817143-3fcf-5599-bdca-2bb39117ba27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9075355b-7512-5362-8a9e-57357160d013" :question/math-academy-id "q-131014" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}$ are solutions to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={x}^{6}$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{6}={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{4}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the products of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}+4x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $4x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+{x}^{2})={x}^{2}+{x}^{4}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fad8b97e-924a-588f-ae21-578df2180d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343827} {:answer/id #uuid "2e641187-eb10-5851-8598-faedcd00898a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x{e}^{x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343828} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbd4043-78d5-5143-90d7-14b147500f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-2x}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343829} {:answer/id #uuid "370ef8d7-7a1c-5d5c-bf91-c561f2638815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343830} {:answer/id #uuid "82eba82e-6539-54f0-8c8c-5f3c78dee6fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82eba82e-6539-54f0-8c8c-5f3c78dee6fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343831} :answer-field/id #uuid "597072c7-e9da-5af9-b902-e0614d3bb99d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c0ea85a-8646-5af0-8c5d-b95cfa962333" :question/math-academy-id "q-131032" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-2y=0,$ what function must also be a solution to the given differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(x)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{e}^{x}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}$ and ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we get:\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{3}=\\frac{2{e}^{x}-{e}^{-2x}}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d72c6e1c-d257-5ad1-93b5-6eb80bc738b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343862} {:answer/id #uuid "bec5a050-34cd-58d0-8449-b9dcabaf82d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343863} {:answer/id #uuid "07c8c2b6-a5e7-53a5-adb2-79404773427d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343864} {:answer/id #uuid "b5209a10-aa98-53b5-895d-ecce39b09eae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343865} {:answer/id #uuid "aaae9815-b868-5a77-aee5-79142c179d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaae9815-b868-5a77-aee5-79142c179d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343866} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3bfc3b-6379-5e8d-ba2c-71c72115635f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ccb0584-4fe2-575d-b3bf-456e92f83038" :question/math-academy-id "q-79774" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-8y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=4x{e}^{4x}$\n- $y(x)=2{e}^{4x}$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{x}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=4x{e}^{4x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=2{e}^{4x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=2.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
